// `print` and `println`, and `eprint` and `eprintln` beside them, whose format
// strings are Rust's.
//
//     println1("x is {}", int(x))
//     println2("{:>8.3} {:#x}", float(ratio), int(mask))
//     eprintln1("cannot read {}", text(name))
//
// The `e` ones write to the error stream and are otherwise the same fn: what a
// line says and where it goes are two questions, and the second is answered by
// which of the four names is written.
//
// What a `{}` may say is what `println!` lets it say -- the argument by number,
// a fill and an alignment, a sign, `#`, a zero flag, a width, a precision, and
// a kind of formatting -- and it means the same thing here, because the same
// grammar is parsed and the value is written out by the `Display`, `Debug`,
// `LowerHex` and the rest that Rust already has. `runtime/src/fmt.rs` is the
// other half of this file and says how, and what a bad format string does.
//
// **Why the arity is in the name.** `println!` takes a format string and
// however many arguments follow it. Nothing here can: a Forte macro takes a
// fixed list of `$name:fragment` and has no repetition (section 2), a fn has no
// variadic form, and two fns may not share a name, so there is no one `println`
// that takes both one argument and three. The number is therefore written on
// the end of the name, and the ladder stops at four because a line with five
// things in it is a line to break in two. Four ladders of five is what that
// comes to, and every rung of them is one call to the runtime.
//
// **Why an argument is wrapped.** `int(x)`, `text(s)` and the three beside them
// make an `Arg`, which is a value with a tag saying what it is. A trait would
// be how this is done in a language that ran the code behind one, and section 8
// says that is what a trait here is still waiting for. Until then the tag is
// the dispatch, and it is written at the call because there is nowhere else to
// write it.

import vec::{Vec, empty, push};

// One thing to print. The tag says which field means anything; the others are
// there because a struct has all of its fields whatever it holds.
//
// It agrees field for field with the `Arg` in `runtime/src/fmt.rs`, which reads
// this through a pointer. Nothing checks that agreement -- it is two programs
// compiled separately, as `shape.rs` says of the descriptors -- so the tags
// below are the numbers that file names and neither set may move alone.
pub struct Arg {
    // Which of the five it is. `pub` for the reason `Vec`'s fields are: there
    // is no visibility that reaches the runtime and not the reader.
    pub tag:  i64,
    // A whole number, an unsigned one read back through its bits, or a bool.
    pub word: i64,
    pub real: f64,
    pub held: str,
}

// ---- Making one -------------------------------------------------------------

pub fn int(n: i64): Arg {
    Arg { tag: 1, word: n, real: 0.0, held: "" }
}

// The bits and not the number: an unsigned value above what an i64 holds comes
// back out of the same bits on the other side, where the tag says to read them
// unsigned. `{}` of one is therefore never a negative number.
pub fn uint(n: u64): Arg {
    Arg { tag: 2, word: n as i64, real: 0.0, held: "" }
}

pub fn float(x: f64): Arg {
    Arg { tag: 3, word: 0, real: x, held: "" }
}

pub fn truth(v: bool): Arg {
    Arg { tag: 4, word: v as i64, real: 0.0, held: "" }
}

pub fn text(s: str): Arg {
    Arg { tag: 5, word: 0, real: 0.0, held: s }
}

// ---- What the runtime does --------------------------------------------------

// One symbol per arity, and one word rather than a flag each for the two
// questions. `how` is 1 for a newline on the end and 2 for the error stream, so
// the four names below are the four numbers 0 to 3.
//
// One word and not two, because six is how many arguments this machine hands
// over in registers: the word, the format string and four `Arg`s is six exactly,
// and a seventh is a call the back end declines to write. Two flags made the
// four-argument rung one of those, and what it left was a `print4` that printed
// nothing at all.
// ---- What crosses to the runtime --------------------------------------------

// One routine, and the number of arguments is a count rather than part of the
// name. `how` is the two flags: 1 for the newline, 2 for the error stream.
//
// A `ptr Arg` and a length rather than a `&Arg[]`, which is what the runtime
// asks for and why is written there: a view is two words and every aggregate
// crosses as an address, so a view would be one more indirection to agree
// about for nothing.
%symbol("__rt_print")
fn out(how: i64, f: str, args: ptr Arg, n: i64): null;

// ---- Printing ---------------------------------------------------------------

// Four routines where there were twenty.
//
// The ladder is gone. It was there because a Forte macro has no repetition and
// a fn has no variadic form, so `print` through `print4` were five spellings of
// one idea and the arity was in the name -- and every argument had to be
// wrapped at the call, `int(x)` and `text(s)`, because there was no way to ask
// a value what it was. Both of those were the same missing thing, and §8 named
// it: "traits with code behind them". There are traits with code behind them
// now.
//
// So what a caller writes is what it means:
//
//     println("x is {}", &[&x])
//     println("{} and {}", &[&name, &count])
//     println("no arguments here", &[])
//
// The `&[..]` is an array of trait objects, borrowed as a view -- which is one
// argument however many things are in it, and is the whole reason the arity
// left the name.
//
// The format string is read even where nothing follows it, so that `{{` is a
// brace here as it is everywhere else and a stray `{}` is reported rather than
// printed.

// What every one of the four does: ask each value for its `Arg`, then hand the
// run of them over. The walk is here and not in the runtime because the runtime
// cannot call into a Forte body -- it is handed values, it does not go and ask
// for them.
//
// `args.len` is what makes this one parameter rather than two. A view has
// carried its length since a reference to an array became a view of it (§3);
// until now nothing could read it, and a caller would have had to count its own
// arguments and be believed.
fn said(how: i64, f: str, args: &(&dyn Show)[]) {
    var held: Vec<Arg> = empty()
    var i = 0
    while i < args.len {
        push(*held, args[i].show())
        i = i + 1
    }
    out(how, f, held.at, held.len)
}

pub fn print(f: str, args: &(&dyn Show)[]) {
    said(0, f, args)
}

pub fn println(f: str, args: &(&dyn Show)[]) {
    said(1, f, args)
}

pub fn eprint(f: str, args: &(&dyn Show)[]) {
    said(2, f, args)
}

pub fn eprintln(f: str, args: &(&dyn Show)[]) {
    said(3, f, args)
}

// ---- What a value looks like when it is written down ------------------------

// Section 8 said what this was waiting for and the head of this file said it
// too: "a trait would be how this is done in a language that ran the code
// behind one, and section 8 says that is what a trait here is still waiting
// for". It is not waiting any more, so the tag a caller used to write is a body
// the value answers with.
//
// **Why it gives back an `Arg` rather than doing the writing.** What formats a
// value is the runtime, in Rust, and the runtime cannot call into a Forte body.
// So the dispatch happens on this side and what crosses the boundary is what
// always crossed it: `Arg` stays the wire format and stops being something a
// caller writes.
//
// **Here and not in a file of its own**, because every impl needs `Arg` and the
// wrappers, and a file of its own would have to import this one while this one
// imported it back.
//
// **Five impls, and exactly five.** They are the five tags the runtime reads
// (`runtime/src/fmt.rs`), and a sixth would have nothing to make an `Arg` out
// of. Not `i32` or `f32`: `int` takes an `i64` and `float` an `f64`, and what a
// narrowing at the call would mean is a question the language has not answered.
//
// Every body here reads its receiver, which is a `&i64` standing where an `i64`
// is wanted (§3). That was the last of the four things a reference is said to
// stand for and the one that was not implemented, and it is why this could not
// have been written before.

pub trait Show {
    fn show(&self): Arg
}

impl Show for i64 {
    fn show(&self): Arg { int(self) }
}

impl Show for u64 {
    fn show(&self): Arg { uint(self) }
}

impl Show for f64 {
    fn show(&self): Arg { float(self) }
}

impl Show for bool {
    fn show(&self): Arg { truth(self) }
}

impl Show for str {
    fn show(&self): Arg { text(self) }
}
